Context: this is a draft (Konzept) of §§17-18 of Riemann's Inaugural Dissertation (Göttingen 1851). In the printed text these become Art. 21, the Riemann mapping theorem, and the start of the following article on behaviour at branch points and boundary curvature. The page continues the proof of the theorem stated on the previous page: every simply connected (possibly branched) surface T can be mapped conformally onto the unit disc K, and the map is fixed once the image of an interior point O (z=z0) and of a boundary point P are prescribed.

Setup from the previous page. Let Θ be a small disc around z0 containing no branch point. Choose a cut l from z0 to the boundary of T. Let α+βi be a complex function of position that:
- equals log(z-z0) in Θ;
- outside Θ is purely imaginary on ∂T;
- jumps by -2πi across l and is otherwise continuous.
Its Dirichlet-type 'defect' integral ∫[(α_x-β_y)^2+(α_y+β_x)^2] dT measures the failure of the Cauchy-Riemann equations. It vanishes on Θ and is finite on the rest of T.

Step 1 (Dirichlet principle, Art. 16/18 of the printed text). Hence one can add a correction to α+βi so that the result is analytic. The correction is uniquely determined up to an imaginary constant and is purely imaginary on the boundary. This gives an analytic function t=m+ni of z with three properties:
- t = log(z-z0) + (holomorphic) near z0;
- Re t = m = 0 on ∂T;
- t jumps by -2πi across l.
In modern terms m = -g(z,z0), where g is the Green's function of T with pole at z0, and n is a harmonic conjugate.

Step 2 (maximum principle, Art. 11). m = 0 on ∂T, m → -∞ at O, and m is harmonic elsewhere. Therefore m ≤ 0 in T, since otherwise m would have an interior maximum.

Step 3 (monotonicity of n on the boundary). Take p as the inner normal and s as arclength in the positive sense, so (s,p) is positively oriented. The Cauchy-Riemann equations then give ∂n/∂s = -∂m/∂p. Since m does not increase inward, ∂m/∂p ≤ 0 on ∂T, hence ∂n/∂s ≥ 0. So n is monotone along the boundary and, because of the -2π jump at l, gains exactly 2π per positive circuit.

Step 4. Put w = e^t. Then:
- w is single valued (the jump 2πi is a period of exp) and finite and continuous on T;
- w ≈ (z-z0)·const near O, so w(O) = 0;
- |w| = e^m = 1 on ∂T, with boundary values w = e^{ni};
- as the boundary is traversed once, n runs once through an interval of length 2π.
Thus the image surface S has a boundary that winds exactly once over the circle K. By Art. 7 (an argument-principle / degree statement), S is a schlicht (single-sheeted) covering of the disc.

Step 5 (normalisation). O is sent to the centre. The free imaginary additive constant in n rotates the disc (w → e^{ic}w), so P can be sent to any prescribed boundary point.

Step 6 (branch point at O). If O is a branch point of order n-1, replace log(z-z0) by (1/n)log(z-z0) and argue the same way, using Art. 14. Details are omitted.

Step 7 (§18 beginning). z as a function of w satisfies the hypotheses of Art. 12. Since S has no branch points, dz/dw is finite everywhere. Similarly dw/dz is finite at all non-branch points of T. The page breaks off at the treatment of branch points; the next page shows dw/d(z-z')^{1/n} is finite there.
